1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,029
Imagine sending a photo to a friend. Your
computer cannot jump

2
00:00:05,029 --> 00:00:10,057
straight into their computer. The photo
travels through connected machines. We

3
00:00:10,057 --> 00:00:15,086
draw each machine as a dot and each connection
as a

4
00:00:15,086 --> 00:00:20,114
line. A small piece of the photo, called a
packet, moves

5
00:00:20,114 --> 00:00:25,143
along those lines. Some machines pass it
onward; these are routers.

6
00:00:25,143 --> 00:00:30,171
For now, forget the big internet. We only need
a starting

7
00:00:30,171 --> 00:00:35,200
dot, an ending dot, and a path between them.

8
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:40,229
Now give each connection a price. These
numbers are made-up points,

9
00:00:40,229 --> 00:00:45,257
so a four costs twice as much as a two. Going

10
00:00:45,257 --> 00:00:50,286
straight from A to B costs four. But watch the
other

11
00:00:50,286 --> 00:00:55,314
way: A to C costs two, then C to B costs

12
00:00:55,314 --> 00:01:00,343
one. Two plus one is three. Taking two steps
was cheaper

13
00:01:00,343 --> 00:01:05,371
than taking one! That is our whole puzzle:
find the route

14
00:01:05,371 --> 00:01:10,400
with the smallest total, rather than the
fewest lines.

15
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,238
Let's add a few more machines. Our starting
point is still

16
00:01:15,238 --> 00:01:20,076
A, but now we want to reach F. The red route

17
00:01:20,076 --> 00:01:24,914
costs four, then five, then six: fifteen
altogether. The green route

18
00:01:24,914 --> 00:01:29,752
takes more steps, yet its prices add to
thirteen. You can

19
00:01:29,752 --> 00:01:34,590
compare these two by eye. On a huge map,
guessing every

20
00:01:34,590 --> 00:01:39,429
route would get messy. We need a repeatable
recipe. That recipe

21
00:01:39,429 --> 00:01:44,267
has a name: Dijkstra's shortest-path
algorithm.

22
00:01:44,267 --> 00:01:49,362
Start with a fresh scorecard beside our map.
Each score means

23
00:01:49,362 --> 00:01:54,457
the cheapest total we have found so far from
A. A

24
00:01:54,457 --> 00:01:59,552
starts at zero because we are already there.
Everywhere else gets

25
00:01:59,552 --> 00:02:04,648
a question mark: we have not found a route
yet. Books

26
00:02:04,648 --> 00:02:09,743
often use this sideways eight, called
infinity, for those unknown scores.

27
00:02:09,743 --> 00:02:14,838
It does not mean the machines are impossibly
far away. It

28
00:02:14,838 --> 00:02:19,933
means we still need to discover how to reach
them.

29
00:02:19,933 --> 00:02:24,900
Look at the lines leaving A. We can reach B
for

30
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:29,867
four points, so write four beside B. We can
reach C

31
00:02:29,867 --> 00:02:34,833
for two points, so write two beside C. Which
unfinished score

32
00:02:34,833 --> 00:02:39,800
is smaller? C, with two. That is where we look
next.

33
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:44,767
We always choose the smallest unfinished
score, then check its neighboring

34
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:49,733
dots. Green will mean a score is finished.
Yellow will mean

35
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:54,700
we have found an improvement worth writing
down.

36
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:59,795
We have reached C for two points. From here, B
costs

37
00:02:59,795 --> 00:03:04,890
only one more. Add them: two plus one makes
three. Our

38
00:03:04,890 --> 00:03:09,986
old score for B was four, so replace it with
three.

39
00:03:09,986 --> 00:03:15,081
We just found the little shortcut from
earlier! Also check C's

40
00:03:15,081 --> 00:03:20,176
other connections. D would cost two plus
eight, or ten. E

41
00:03:20,176 --> 00:03:25,271
would cost two plus ten, or twelve. Write
those down as

42
00:03:25,271 --> 00:03:30,367
possible routes. A score can improve before it
becomes final.

43
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:34,933
Our unfinished scores are now three at B, ten
at D,

44
00:03:34,933 --> 00:03:39,500
and twelve at E. Choose B because three is the
smallest.

45
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:44,067
From B, the connection to D costs five. We
have already

46
00:03:44,067 --> 00:03:48,633
spent three getting to B, so the whole journey
to D

47
00:03:48,633 --> 00:03:53,200
costs eight. Eight beats the old ten. Cross
out ten and

48
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:57,767
write eight. Notice that we are adding to the
journey so

49
00:03:57,767 --> 00:04:02,333
far, not choosing a connection only because
its own price looks

50
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:06,900
small.

51
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:11,929
D now has the smallest unfinished score:
eight. Finish D and

52
00:04:11,929 --> 00:04:16,957
look outward. Reaching E through D costs eight
plus two, which

53
00:04:16,957 --> 00:04:21,986
is ten. That improves E's old twelve. Reaching
F through D

54
00:04:21,986 --> 00:04:27,014
costs eight plus six, which is fourteen. At
last, we have

55
00:04:27,014 --> 00:04:32,043
found a route to our destination! But wait
before celebrating. Fourteen

56
00:04:32,043 --> 00:04:37,071
is the best answer we have found so far.
Another unfinished

57
00:04:37,071 --> 00:04:42,100
dot might still lead us to a cheaper route.

58
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:47,257
The next smallest unfinished score is E at
ten. Its connection

59
00:04:47,257 --> 00:04:52,414
to F costs three. Ten plus three is thirteen,
beating the

60
00:04:52,414 --> 00:04:57,571
fourteen we just wrote down. Replace it. This
is why finding

61
00:04:57,571 --> 00:05:02,729
the destination once was not enough. The first
route we noticed

62
00:05:02,729 --> 00:05:07,886
was useful, but it was not the winner. Our
rule stayed

63
00:05:07,886 --> 00:05:13,043
exactly the same: choose the smallest
unfinished score, add the next

64
00:05:13,043 --> 00:05:18,200
connection's price, and keep the result only
when it improves something.

65
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:22,767
F now has the smallest unfinished score,
thirteen. When we choose

66
00:05:22,767 --> 00:05:27,333
it, that score becomes final. We can stop if F
was

67
00:05:27,333 --> 00:05:31,900
our only destination. If we wanted answers for
every machine, we

68
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:36,467
would keep following the same rule until all
reachable dots were

69
00:05:36,467 --> 00:05:41,033
finished. The changing numbers were not random
guesses. Each one described

70
00:05:41,033 --> 00:05:45,600
a real route we had found, and each
replacement made that

71
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:50,167
route cheaper. Green marks the point where we
know it cannot

72
00:05:50,167 --> 00:05:54,733
improve.

73
00:05:54,733 --> 00:05:59,300
Why trust the smallest unfinished score? Every
connection in our picture

74
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:03,867
adds a positive cost. Suppose the cheapest
unfinished dot costs eight.

75
00:06:03,867 --> 00:06:08,433
A route that first goes through an unfinished
dot costing ten

76
00:06:08,433 --> 00:06:13,000
cannot come back and beat eight; adding more
positive prices only

77
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,567
makes ten larger. That is the key idea behind
the recipe.

78
00:06:17,567 --> 00:06:22,133
Zero-cost connections also work. Negative
prices would break this reasoning, so

79
00:06:22,133 --> 00:06:26,700
this version of the algorithm requires costs
that never go below

80
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:31,267
zero.

81
00:06:31,267 --> 00:06:36,362
A score tells us the price, but how do we
recover

82
00:06:36,362 --> 00:06:41,457
the actual route? Whenever a score improves,
remember which dot supplied

83
00:06:41,457 --> 00:06:46,552
that improvement. F remembers E. E remembers
D. D remembers B.

84
00:06:46,552 --> 00:06:51,648
B remembers C, and C remembers A. Follow those
reminders backward

85
00:06:51,648 --> 00:06:56,743
and you arrive home. Now reverse the list to
get the

86
00:06:56,743 --> 00:07:01,838
travel directions. There is no need to try all
the routes

87
00:07:01,838 --> 00:07:06,933
again. We saved just enough information while
improving the numbers.

88
00:07:06,933 --> 00:07:12,090
Let's take the journey ourselves: A to C, C to
B,

89
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:17,248
B to D, D to E, and E to F. The

90
00:07:17,248 --> 00:07:22,405
connection prices are two, one, five, two, and
three. Keep a

91
00:07:22,405 --> 00:07:27,562
running total as the packet moves: two, three,
eight, ten, thirteen.

92
00:07:27,562 --> 00:07:32,719
Five steps beat the three-step route that cost
fifteen. A drawing

93
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:37,876
can make one line look short and another look
long. The

94
00:07:37,876 --> 00:07:43,033
numbers, rather than the shape of our drawing,
decide the winner.

95
00:07:43,033 --> 00:07:48,190
Now remove the connection between D and E. The
old winning

96
00:07:48,190 --> 00:07:53,348
route runs straight into a missing line. We
cannot repair it

97
00:07:53,348 --> 00:07:58,505
simply by pretending the old answer is still
true. Update the

98
00:07:58,505 --> 00:08:03,662
map and run the same recipe again. Some scores
may stay

99
00:08:03,662 --> 00:08:08,819
the same, because their routes never needed
that connection. Others will

100
00:08:08,819 --> 00:08:13,976
change. This is a useful networking lesson: a
good set of

101
00:08:13,976 --> 00:08:19,133
directions depends on an accurate picture of
which connections are available.

102
00:08:19,133 --> 00:08:23,812
With that line gone, try A to C to B to

103
00:08:23,812 --> 00:08:28,492
D to F. Its prices add to two plus one plus

104
00:08:28,492 --> 00:08:33,171
five plus six: fourteen. The route through C
and E costs

105
00:08:33,171 --> 00:08:37,850
two plus ten plus three: fifteen. Fourteen
wins. The updated calculation

106
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:42,529
gives E a score of twelve and F a score of

107
00:08:42,529 --> 00:08:47,208
fourteen. We did not invent a special rule for
broken connections.

108
00:08:47,208 --> 00:08:51,888
We changed the map, then let the same small
comparisons find

109
00:08:51,888 --> 00:08:56,567
the new answer.

110
00:08:56,567 --> 00:09:01,192
We began with one message and a few connected
dots. A

111
00:09:01,192 --> 00:09:05,817
line had a price, a route added those prices,
and a

112
00:09:05,817 --> 00:09:10,442
score remembered the best total found so far.
Dijkstra's method chose

113
00:09:10,442 --> 00:09:15,067
the cheapest unfinished score and improved its
neighbors, one step at

114
00:09:15,067 --> 00:09:19,692
a time. That simple routine revealed a whole
route. So next

115
00:09:19,692 --> 00:09:24,317
time a winding path beats a tidy shortcut, it
will not

116
00:09:24,317 --> 00:09:28,942
feel mysterious. More steps can cost less when
the steps themselves

117
00:09:28,942 --> 00:09:33,567
are cheaper.
